src/backend/storage/file/README

Transactional File Operations (FILEOPS)
=======================================

This directory's fileops.c and fileops_undo.c implement FILEOPS: a set of
filesystem operations that participate in PostgreSQL transactions.  An
ordinary unlink() or rename() issued in the middle of a transaction is not
covered by WAL and is not reversed if the transaction aborts or the server
crashes.  FILEOPS closes that gap for the filesystem mutations the server
performs outside the buffer manager -- creating and removing database and
tablespace directories, copying directory trees, writing version files,
managing symlinks, and setting extended attributes.


Why FILEOPS exists
------------------

PostgreSQL already makes heap and index changes crash-safe by WAL-logging
them and replaying the log during recovery.  Directory- and file-level
operations historically had no equivalent.  Code paths such as CREATE
DATABASE, CREATE TABLESPACE, and ALTER DATABASE ... SET TABLESPACE perform
raw mkdir(), symlink(), rename(), and rmtree() calls and then rely on
ad-hoc cleanup callbacks to undo their effects on failure.  That cleanup is
best-effort: it does not survive a crash, and it is easy to get the abort
ordering wrong.

FILEOPS gives these operations the same two guarantees the rest of the
system enjoys:

  Atomicity with the transaction.  A FILEOPS request does not touch the
  filesystem when it is issued.  It is recorded as a deferred PendingFileOp
  and executed only when the transaction commits (or, for delete-on-abort
  registrations, when it aborts).  If the transaction never commits, the
  on-disk state is never changed.

  Crash safety.  Each executed operation is WAL-logged under the
  RM_FILEOPS_ID resource manager, so redo during crash recovery reproduces
  it on the primary, and a physical standby reproduces it by WAL replay.
  This holds for the immediate operations (CREATE, WRITE, LINK, MKDIR,
  CHMOD, CHOWN, TRUNCATE, SETXATTR, REMOVEXATTR) and, as of the B3 fix, for
  the deferred DESTRUCTIVE operations (RENAME, DELETE, RMDIR) as well: their
  WAL record is emitted at commit-EXECUTION time inside FileOpsDoPendingOps()
  -- when the op actually happens on the primary -- and their redo handlers
  are now real and idempotent (a delete of an already-absent file, a rename
  whose source is already gone, or an rmdir of an already-absent directory
  all count as success), so the standby converges regardless of a primary
  crash mid-commit.  Only the commit path is logged; a delete-on-abort /
  rmdir-on-abort registration executed during ABORT emits no WAL, because a
  standby must never apply an aborted transaction's filesystem effect.  See
  src/test/recovery/t/070_fileops_standby_divergence.pl for the
  physical-standby diff test that gates this.

  Two deliberate exceptions:

    RMTREE (FileOpsRmtree) writes no WAL and has no redo.  Its only core
    caller (copydir.c) registers it at_commit=false, i.e. it fires only on
    ABORT to clean up a partially-copied destination tree; aborts are never
    replayed as committed, so it cannot diverge a standby.  A commit-time
    RMTREE would NOT be standby-safe and is currently unused.

    SYMLINK redo is an intentional no-op.  FileOpsSymlink is used ONLY for
    tablespace symlinks (create_tablespace_directories), and standby /
    crash-recovery replay of those is owned by RM_TBLSPC, which applies the
    recovery-time layout remap.  Replaying the FILEOPS record would be
    redundant and wrong on a remapped standby (see sec2 in fileops.c).

  Operations that must be reversible on rollback
  also emit UNDO records (RM_UNDO_ID, subtypes FILEOPS_UNDO_*) carrying the
  before-image needed to reverse them -- the original mode for a chmod, the
  original length for a truncate, the prior xattr value for a setxattr, and
  so on.

The operation set deliberately follows the Berkeley DB fileops.src model:
every filesystem mutation is a composable unit with its own WAL record
type, redo handler, and rmgrdesc descriptor, so each can be replayed
independently.


How to use the API
------------------

The public API is declared in src/include/storage/fileops.h.  Call these in
place of the raw libc/syscall equivalents whenever the operation must be
transactional.  Each returns the same way its libc counterpart does (the
create/rename/chmod/chown/mkdir/symlink/link calls return an int errno-style
result; the void calls register a pending op):

  FileOpsCreate(path, flags, mode, register_delete)
  FileOpsDelete(path, at_commit)
  FileOpsRename(oldpath, newpath)
  FileOpsWrite(path, offset, data, len)
  FileOpsTruncate(path, length)
  FileOpsChmod(path, mode)
  FileOpsChown(path, uid, gid)
  FileOpsMkdir(path, mode)
  FileOpsRmdir(path, at_commit)
  FileOpsRmdirRecursive(path, at_commit)
  FileOpsRmtree(path, at_commit)
  FileOpsSymlink(target, linkpath)
  FileOpsLink(oldpath, newpath)
  FileOpsSetXattr(path, name, value, len)
  FileOpsRemoveXattr(path, name)

Two flags recur in this API and are worth understanding:

  register_delete (FileOpsCreate).  When true, the newly created file is
  registered for deletion if the transaction aborts.  This is the common
  case for files a transaction brings into existence: keep them on commit,
  remove them on abort.

  at_commit (FileOpsDelete, FileOpsRmdir, FileOpsRmdirRecursive,
  FileOpsRmtree).  Selects when the removal happens.  at_commit = true means
  "remove this when the transaction commits" -- the right choice for tearing
  down state the transaction is logically deleting (for example, dropping a
  database directory).  at_commit = false means "remove this only if the
  transaction aborts" -- used to clean up something the transaction just
  created, such as the destination tree of an in-progress directory copy.

FileOpsRmtree differs from FileOpsRmdirRecursive in when the tree is walked.
FileOpsRmdirRecursive enumerates the directory's contents at registration
time, so it removes what is present now.  FileOpsRmtree defers the walk to
execute time, so it removes whatever ended up on disk by commit/abort --
the correct choice when registering the destination of a copydir() that is
still being populated.

The API hides platform differences: PG_O_DIRECT for unbuffered I/O,
pg_fsync() for the correct per-platform flush, fsync_parent_path() for
directory durability on Unix, and durable_rename()/durable_unlink() for
crash-safe rename and unlink ordering.


Transaction lifecycle integration
----------------------------------

The deferred operations are driven by hooks that the transaction machinery
(src/backend/access/transam/xact.c) calls at the right points.  Callers of
the FileOps* API do not invoke these directly; they are documented here so
the lifecycle is clear:

  FileOpsDoPendingOps(isCommit).  Executes the pending operation list at
  end of transaction.  Called with true from the commit path and with false
  from the abort path; each PendingFileOp's at_commit flag decides whether
  it fires in this phase.

  AtSubCommit_FileOps().  On subtransaction commit, reparents the
  subtransaction's pending operations to the parent so they are decided by
  the parent's eventual commit or abort.

  AtSubAbort_FileOps().  On subtransaction abort, executes and discards the
  pending operations belonging to the aborting subtransaction.

  PostPrepare_FileOps().  At PREPARE TRANSACTION, hands the pending
  operations off so they are resolved when the prepared transaction is later
  committed or rolled back.

  FileOpsCancelPendingDelete(path, at_commit).  Removes a previously
  registered delete-on-commit or delete-on-abort entry, for the case where
  a caller decides the file should no longer be removed.

  FileOpsSync(path).  Forces durability of a path outside the deferred
  queue when an operation needs to be flushed immediately.


WAL replay and UNDO
-------------------

fileops.c provides the redo side: fileops_redo() dispatches each
XLOG_FILEOPS_* record to the matching action during recovery, and
fileops_desc()/fileops_identify() render records for pg_waldump.  Because
the redo handler is itself the recovery action, code paths that run under
WAL replay (the "redo" arms in dbcommands.c, tablespace.c, and copydir.c)
call the raw syscalls directly rather than re-entering the FileOps* API.

fileops_undo.c provides the rollback side.  For operations whose effect must
be reversed when a transaction aborts, it records a FILEOPS_UNDO_* payload
holding the before-image and, during UNDO application, performs the inverse
action: unlink a created file, rename back, restore the original truncation
length, mode, owner, or extended-attribute value.  FileopsUndoRmgrInit()
registers this handler with the UNDO machinery at startup.
